---
import Site from '../../../layouts/Site.astro';
import StudyHeader from '../../../components/StudyHeader.astro';
import DecisionBrief from '../../../components/DecisionBrief.astro';
import StudyEvidence from '../../../components/StudyEvidence.astro';
import TelecomExplorer from '../../../components/TelecomExplorer';
import { studies } from '../../../data/studies';
import telecom from '../../../data/telecom-overview.json';
import '../../../styles/allocation-studies.css';
const reference=telecom.default_case;
const result=reference.policies.optimized.summary;
const adaptive=reference.policies.adaptive.summary;
const fixed=reference.policies.fixed.summary;
const all=telecom.evaluationSummary.againstBaselines.adaptive;
const fmt=(v:number,d=0)=>new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB',{maximumFractionDigits:d}).format(v);
---
<Site title={studies.telecom.title} description="Explore integer staff allocation across telecom complaint workstreams using open FCC intake, hypothetical processing capacity and complete simulated backlog comparisons.">
 <StudyHeader study={studies.telecom}/>
 <DecisionBrief id="telecom-model" title="The staff allocation decision" items={[
  {label:'The decision',text:'At the start of each week, allocate whole cross-trained staff to billing/commercial, service/technical and privacy casework. Specialists remain in their own queue. Nobody can be assigned twice.'},
  {label:'The objective',text:'First minimize the largest projected unprocessed share across the three queues. Then minimize total projected remaining cases, followed by internal staff reallocations. This balances coverage; it does not minimize total backlog over twelve weeks.'},
  {label:'The information',text:'Use earlier observed complaint intake plus the policy’s own simulated opening backlog. Forecast history is selected on an earlier validation period. The later week’s observed intake evaluates the allocation.'},
  {label:'The constraints',text:'The total team and cross-trained pool are fixed by the scenario. Whole-case processing cannot exceed the cases available or hypothetical capacity. Unprocessed cases carry into the next week, oldest model cohorts first.'},
 ]}>
  <p class="model-control-explanation"><strong>Staff and processing capacity are scenario assumptions.</strong> At the base setting, specialists have 60 billing, 40 technical or 30 privacy processing slots per week. Cross-trained staff operate at 80% of those rates. The remaining specialist team follows a hypothetical 45% / 45% / 10% split, rounded to whole people.</p>
  <details class="data-details model-formulation"><summary>Variables, constraints and comparison policies</summary><p>Choose integer cross-trained assignments <var>x</var><sub>q</sub> whose sum equals the available flexible pool. Dedicated counts <var>s</var><sub>q</sub> are fixed. Queue capacity is the floor of the assumed base rate × capacity multiplier × (specialists + 0.8 × cross-trained staff).</p><ul class="model-equations"><li>∑ x<sub>q</sub> = available cross-trained staff</li><li>Projected work<sub>q</sub> = own opening backlog<sub>q</sub> + forecast intake<sub>q</sub></li><li>Projected residual share<sub>q</sub> = max(projected work<sub>q</sub> − capacity<sub>q</sub>, 0) / projected work<sub>q</sub></li><li>Ending backlog = opening backlog + observed intake − simulated processed cases</li></ul><p>An empty queue has zero residual share. Exhaustive enumeration of all three-queue integer compositions gives an exact weekly decision; rational comparisons avoid rounding the objective. Equal outcomes prefer fewer internal staff moves, then queue order. The first week has no previous allocation to move from.</p><p>The fixed rule uses training intake divided by each queue’s assumed processing rate. The adaptive rule apportions flexible staff to projected work remaining after specialist capacity. Both have the same pool, skills and forecast information. Each policy carries its own backlog; their later starting states therefore differ.</p><p>The current-week hindsight reference substitutes actual intake for the forecast but still makes a weekly decision and carries its own backlog. It is unavailable in advance and is not a lower bound on the whole twelve-week problem.</p></details>
  <p class="model-scope"><strong>Scope:</strong> consumer-submitted Phone/Internet complaints to the US Federal Communications Commission. This is a hypothetical team processing casework drawn from regulator intake, not a telecommunications operator’s contact centre. Staffing, skills, effort, backlog and processing are modelled; no measured resolution time, SLA or labour saving is claimed.</p>
 </DecisionBrief>
 <section class="section tint" id="telecom-explorer"><div class="shell"><div class="explorer-intro"><h2>Move the staff. Follow the remaining work.</h2><p>Choose team size, cross-training and assumed capacity. The model allocates the flexible pool each week. Inspect where staff go, how much work carries forward and how the simple rules compare.</p><p><a href="#telecom-model">Review the decision and assumptions ↑</a></p></div><TelecomExplorer index={telecom} client:load/><noscript><p>The reference trajectory is shown. Enable JavaScript to change staffing scenarios, policies and week detail, or inspect all scenarios in the Colab notebook below.</p></noscript><p class="source-credit">{telecom.source.credit} {telecom.source.boundary} Independent model: Abdulaziz Aldoseri.</p></div></section>
 <section class="section shell study-prose" id="findings"><p class="eyebrow">What the evidence supports</p><h2>More balanced coverage can leave more total work.</h2><p>In the predefined 48-person, 25% cross-trained, base-capacity scenario, balancing queue coverage reduces the mean worst unprocessed share to {fmt(result.meanWorstShare*100,2)}%, compared with {fmt(adaptive.meanWorstShare*100,2)}% for the adaptive rule and {fmt(fixed.meanWorstShare*100,2)}% for the fixed rule.</p><p>That balance has a throughput trade-off. It ends with {fmt(result.endingBacklog)} simulated cases remaining: {fmt(result.endingBacklog-adaptive.endingBacklog)} more than the adaptive rule and {fmt(result.endingBacklog-fixed.endingBacklog)} more than the fixed rule. Its total backlog is higher on all twelve reference weeks. The model achieves its weekly coverage objective; this does not make it the best policy for minimizing the total backlog.</p><p>Across all 72 staffing settings, coverage balancing has a higher cumulative backlog than the adaptive rule in {all.higherCumulativeBacklogScenarios}, a lower one in {all.lowerCumulativeBacklogScenarios}, and ties in {all.equalCumulativeBacklogScenarios}. Its mean worst-queue share is worse in {all.higherMeanWorstShareScenarios} settings. All scenarios remain available; the results are not retuned to favour the method.</p><div class="scope-note"><p>The operating choice depends on what the team values: balanced coverage, total throughput, specialist depth and flexibility. Those priorities must be made explicit before choosing an allocation rule. The source does not supply the real staffing rates needed to turn this demonstration into an operational recommendation.</p></div></section>
 <section class="section tint" id="method"><div class="shell study-prose"><p class="eyebrow">Evaluation &amp; preparation</p><h2>Separate recorded intake from simulated capacity.</h2><div class="method-grid"><article><h3>Define the casework</h3><p>The retrieved Phone/Internet scope contains 205,892 unique complaints. A fixed issue mapping admits 102,757 billing, technical and privacy records. It excludes 100,600 unwanted calls, 2,433 junk faxes and 102 unclassified issues. Raw complaint IDs are not public assets.</p></article><article><h3>Keep time in order</h3><p>Sixteen training weeks are followed by eight validation weeks and twelve test weeks. UTC submission timestamps define complete Monday-start weeks. The test is 23 June–14 September 2025; policies see only completed earlier intake before deciding.</p></article><article><h3>Preserve each trajectory</h3><p>Every policy starts with zero simulated backlog and retains its own unprocessed cohorts. All 3,456 policy-week records and separate stress cases are preserved. Conservation, staffing integrality, FIFO processing and exhaustive allocation checks were independently tested.</p></article></div>
 <details class="data-details"><summary>Forecast selection and its weaker test performance</summary><p>Validation selected the previous-week forecast. The held-out period later favoured a longer average on forecast error; that diagnostic does not change the selected policy. All four candidates are shown below without test-period reselection.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabIndex="0" role="region" aria-label="Telecom forecast evaluation"><table><caption>Mean absolute intake error · cases per queue-week · lower is better</caption><thead><tr><th>Previous complete weeks</th><th>Validation MAE</th><th>Test MAE</th></tr></thead><tbody>{telecom.forecastValidation.map(v=><tr><th scope="row">{v.window}{v.window===telecom.selectedForecastWindow?' · selected':''}</th><td>{fmt(v.mae,2)}</td><td>{fmt(telecom.forecastTest.find(t=>t.window===v.window)!.mae,2)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></details>
 <details class="data-details"><summary>Capacity and disruption stress checks</summary><p>These are explicitly modified model scenarios, separate from the primary observed-intake replay.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabIndex="0" role="region" aria-label="Telecom staffing stress cases"><table><caption>Coverage-balancing policy · final simulated backlog, cases</caption><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Cases remaining</th></tr></thead><tbody>{[
 ['Reference · 48 staff, 25% cross-trained',result.endingBacklog],
 ['No staff',telecom.stressSummaries.zero_staff.policies.optimized.endingBacklog],
 ['No intake, zero opening backlog',telecom.stressSummaries.zero_intake.policies.optimized.endingBacklog],
 ['240 staff · ample-capacity check',telecom.stressSummaries.ample_staff.policies.optimized.endingBacklog],
 ['One cross-trained staff member absent for the week of 4 August',telecom.stressSummaries.flex_absence.policies.optimized.endingBacklog],
 ['Technical specialists unavailable',telecom.stressSummaries.technical_specialists_absent.policies.optimized.endingBacklog],
 ['48 staff · all cross-trained at 80% efficiency',telecom.stressSummaries.all_cross_trained.policies.optimized.endingBacklog],
 ].map(([label,value])=><tr><th scope="row">{label}</th><td>{fmt(Number(value))}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>Flexibility is not free in this formulation: replacing specialists with cross-trained staff changes capacity through the assumed 80% efficiency. All raw stress trajectories, including an intake surge and an initial backlog, are in the reproduction package.</p></details>
 <p class="scope-note">The FCC records consumer-selected allegations and intake, not independently verified faults, employee workload or case outcomes. Historical public API availability and revisions are not observed. This retrospective replay assumes earlier submissions are visible to an internal planner. The period precedes an identified October source discontinuity; missing records are not treated as a staffing success.</p></div></section>
 <StudyEvidence title="Open complaint intake. Reproducible staff allocation." notebook="telecom"><Fragment slot="description"><p>{telecom.source.credit} Retrieved 14 September 2026. The source covers 6 January–14 September 2025. The public package contains aggregate daily/weekly counts, preparation and model code, the frozen protocol, tests and complete evaluated results. It excludes individual complaint IDs.</p></Fragment><p><a href={telecom.source.url}>Original FCC dataset and reuse terms</a></p><p class="source-credit">FCC metadata identifies this dataset as US Government public-domain work. Queue grouping and aggregate/model outputs are independent transformations, not FCC staffing guidance or endorsement.</p></StudyEvidence>
</Site>
